Re: Exmh && speed,"Valdis.Kletnieks@vt.edu said: > I checked on a number of small messages in a big folder (~10000 > msgs). The delay of the Next button has increased considerably: > > 2.5-release: 350-450 msec > latest cvs: 1000-12000 msec > I'm not seeing a hit on 'next'. A quick ""just pound on 'next' and > watch the wall clock"" test shows me able to go through 20 messages in > under 5 seconds, so it's well under 250ms per switch, but I'm seeing a > really piggy CPU spike (100% for a few seconds) in the 'flist' code. > Of course, it seems to be related to number-of-folders: > [~] wc Mail/.folders > 131 131 1122 Mail/.folders I have 167 folders (!) and run with bg-proc set to flist (1 minute). I see delays, but not that much. Maybe 1-3 seconds (which tend to disappear these days). This is on a PII-266. BR, /A _______________________________________________ Exmh-workers mailing list Exmh-workers@redhat.com https://listman.redhat.com/mailman/listinfo/exmh-workers","Re: Exmh && speed Valdis.Kletnieks@vt.edu said: > I checked on a number of small messages in a big folder (~10000 > msgs). The delay of the Next button has increased considerably: > > 2.5-release: 350-450 msec > latest cvs: 1000-12000 msec > I'm not seeing a hit on 'next'. A quick ""just pound on 'next' and > watch the wall clock"" test shows me able to go through 20 messages in > under 5 seconds, so it's well under 250ms per switch, but I'm seeing a > really piggy CPU spike (100% for a few seconds) in the 'flist' code. > Of course, it seems to be related to number-of-folders: > [~] wc Mail/.folders > 131 131 1122 Mail/.folders I have 167 folders (!) and run with bg-proc set to flist (1 minute). I see delays, but not that much. Maybe 1-3 seconds (which tend to disappear these days). This is on a PII-266. BR, /A _______________________________________________ Exmh-workers mailing list Exmh-workers@redhat.com https://listman.redhat.com/mailman/listinfo/exmh-workers",0,20030228_easy_ham.tar.bz2,easy_ham/009