"Hey, thief! This laptop is locked (Tech Update)","Tech Update Today VITAL SIGNS FOR JULY 19, 2002 David Berlind Hey, thief! This laptop is locked Try stealing my notebook and Caveo Anti-Theft will yell at you louder and louder -- before resorting to an unstoppable, ear-piercing screech. It's motion detection on a PC Card. And it's virtually foolproof Also: Start-up has locks for Secure Notebook Latest from ZDNet News Earnings: Breaking the bottom line Feds set to enforce PC security Movement grows to boost Wi-Fi hot spots States may be blocking e-commerce Microsoft's chief lawyer calls it quits Will Mac users pay for 'Mac experience'? HP in top PC slot--for now More Enterprise News Farber's Picks Dan Farber Active Directory unbundled Microsoft will begin selling its directory technology as a standalone product separate from Windows. The move could be seen as Microsoft's attempt to satisfy those that have accused the software giant of monopolistic practices for integrating a multitude of software into its operating system. Sun and Novell already offer so-called lightweight directory software, so Microsoft is joining an already thriving marketplace for products that store and manage information about users, computer systems and networking devices. Read the full story How much do you build on Linux? A question for IT managers is whether Linux is ready to move beyond file and Web servers to application and Web services servers. If IBM, Oracle, BEA, Sun, HP and other vendors have a say, the answer is yes. Find out why CRM's dirty little secret The goal of CRM is to reduce costs, increase revenues, and boost customer loyalty. But what if your data is dirty ? Tech Update's Adrian Mello has tips on how companies can cut down on inaccurate and potentially damaging customer data. Clean up your act Get your IT budgets approved An experienced IT manager offers eight tips for getting budgets approved--plus a sample budget that shows his recommendations in action. Does your company have a five-year plan for its IT budget? Read the full story How a CTO faced his worst nightmare After Sept. 11, investors worried whether the stock market would quickly get back to business. NYSE technology chief Roger Burkhardt was on the job faster than a trader can say ""buy."" But now Burkhardt and his technology team face a new era of instability marked by fears of terrorist attacks, corporate accounting scandals, and a stock market meltdown. CNET News.com recently spoke with Burkhardt about the technology challenges the NYSE regularly faces because of the whipsaw movements of the stock market. Read the full interview In what business do we trust? In response to my recent column, "" Desperately seeking chief ethics officer ,"" David Boulton writes: ""We are missing the point when we focus on the executives of corporations and miss the underlying ethic of business in general."" Boulton asks how we can possibly trust institutions that reduce human beings ""to assets whose behaviors are fair game to manipulate?"" Read his call for a new ethic Back to top Also on Tech Update Today FEATURE New connections reshape the PC Big changes are coming in PC design. Meta Group thinks enterprises should begin to plan how these changes will affect existing projects and purchasing plans. COMMENTARY The road to the real tech recovery Have falling stock prices and the tech meltdown got you down? Eric Knorr argues that the road to the real tech recovery is paved with back-to-basics innovation. PREVIOUSLY ON TECH UPDATE TODAY Gaga for Google? When results don't count When Dan Farber's Web search on a leading IT exec came up almost empty, he was forced to rethink his use of search engines. Crucial Clicks--products worth looking at MONITORS A Porsche you can afford Samsung paired up with F.A. Porsche designers to deliver the SyncMaster 171P, a sleek, 17-inch LCD. Read review Most Popular Products Monitors 1 . Samsung SyncMaster 151S 2 . NEC MultiSync 1550V 3 . Envision EN-5100e 4