Re: [ILUG] Seconds to date?,"On Sun, Sep 01, 2002 at 04:14:17PM +0100, Paul Jakma mentioned: > On Sun, 1 Sep 2002, kevin lyda wrote: > > > gnu date is limited by time_t. but i thought time_t expired in 2037? > > this seems to show it expiring in 2038: > > (2^31-1)/3600/24/365+1970 > 2038 > > course, on UltraSparc, x86-64, IA64, alpha, etc: > > (2^63-1)/3600/24/365+1970 > 292471210647 > > so we should be safe enough. May I assume that x86-64 will be able to use a 64bit time_t too? Kate -- Irish Linux Users' Group: ilug@linux.ie http://www.linux.ie/mailman/listinfo/ilug for (un)subscription information. List maintainer: listmaster@linux.ie","Re: [ILUG] Seconds to date? On Sun, Sep 01, 2002 at 04:14:17PM +0100, Paul Jakma mentioned: > On Sun, 1 Sep 2002, kevin lyda wrote: > > > gnu date is limited by time_t. but i thought time_t expired in 2037? > > this seems to show it expiring in 2038: > > (2^31-1)/3600/24/365+1970 > 2038 > > course, on UltraSparc, x86-64, IA64, alpha, etc: > > (2^63-1)/3600/24/365+1970 > 292471210647 > > so we should be safe enough. May I assume that x86-64 will be able to use a 64bit time_t too? Kate -- Irish Linux Users' Group: ilug@linux.ie http://www.linux.ie/mailman/listinfo/ilug for (un)subscription information. List maintainer: listmaster@linux.ie",0,20030228_easy_ham.tar.bz2,easy_ham/00084.2ef0c65e298880c3869b39dc95b40e8e,0d1cd58adc78a519a6190d748011776ab0cf5d3b31c63fcc7107e19c719cfa88
