RE: [ILUG] slashdot EW Dijkstra humor,"On Mon, 2002-08-12 at 19:56, Kenn Humborg wrote: > Well, you should consider 'break' inside a 'select' statement > to be part of the syntax, rather than a control flow modifier. Why? When it comes down to the assembly level, it gets turned into some kind of jump instruction. Should I pretend I don't know this when I'm programming? > A _mandatory_ part of the syntax, at that. It's not mandatory. You can leave out the break, and the flow of control will continue to the next case. Which can be useful in certain circumstances. Nick -- Irish Linux Users' Group: ilug@linux.ie http://www.linux.ie/mailman/listinfo/ilug for (un)subscription information. List maintainer: listmaster@linux.ie","RE: [ILUG] slashdot EW Dijkstra humor On Mon, 2002-08-12 at 19:56, Kenn Humborg wrote: > Well, you should consider 'break' inside a 'select' statement > to be part of the syntax, rather than a control flow modifier. Why? When it comes down to the assembly level, it gets turned into some kind of jump instruction. Should I pretend I don't know this when I'm programming? > A _mandatory_ part of the syntax, at that. It's not mandatory. You can leave out the break, and the flow of control will continue to the next case. Which can be useful in certain circumstances. Nick -- Irish Linux Users' Group: ilug@linux.ie http://www.linux.ie/mailman/listinfo/ilug for (un)subscription information. List maintainer: listmaster@linux.ie",0,20030228_easy_ham_2.tar.bz2,easy_ham_2/00353.3cb4c058f609b8e464be92437be6f81b,ba6f97ee4fbed0a23e48d1b02c8a6d54256a3ef5b93e7282d882ddeef09c5004
