RE: [SAtalk] O.T. Habeus -- Why?,"> I think I've worked on SA enough to understand that I can localize a > score. I'm just not comfortable with using SpamAssassin as a vehicle > for drumming up your business at the expense of our user base. This is exactly what I think. SpamAssassin has always been conservative about adding unproven RBLs and such, and this should be the same. > I think it would make more sense to start Habeas with a less aggressive > score (one which will not give spammers a quick path into everyone's > inbox) and after we've seen evidence that the system works, then we can > increase the magnitude of the score. I say start it with a zero score and put it in 70_cvs_rules_under_test like any other unproven rule. Then score it based on actual results, not promises. My corpus does not yet contain a single non-spam (or spam) message with a Habeas mark. Based on that, it doesn't impress me and it wouldn't impress the GA either. Rules with exactly the same statistics are being dropped from SA right now, and I don't see why this should be any different. -- Michael Moncur mgm at starlingtech.com http://www.starlingtech.com/ ""Furious activity is no substitute for understanding."" --H. H. Williams ------------------------------------------------------- This sf.net email is sponsored by:ThinkGeek Welcome to geek heaven. http://thinkgeek.com/sf _______________________________________________ Spamassassin-talk mailing list Spamassassin-talk@lists.sourceforge.net https://lists.sourceforge.net/lists/listinfo/spamassassin-talk","RE: [SAtalk] O.T. Habeus -- Why? > I think I've worked on SA enough to understand that I can localize a > score. I'm just not comfortable with using SpamAssassin as a vehicle > for drumming up your business at the expense of our user base. This is exactly what I think. SpamAssassin has always been conservative about adding unproven RBLs and such, and this should be the same. > I think it would make more sense to start Habeas with a less ag