Re: How unlucky can you get?,"Jim Whitehead wrote: > So, after shutting off the water and mopping up, I was left to ponder what > are the odds of having mechanical failure of a large rectangular porcelain > bowl, in the absence of any visible stressors (like someone striking it with > a sledgehammer)? We hadn't done anything unusual to the toilet in the recent All it takes is an overtorqued nut (e.g. at the water intake entrance) to stress the porcelain, and you've got a time bomb waiting to go off. Of course, if you're lucky, you'll overtorque it enough that the tank will break right away, while you still have the water intake shut off. - Joe","Re: How unlucky can you get? Jim Whitehead wrote: > So, after shutting off the water and mopping up, I was left to ponder what > are the odds of having mechanical failure of a large rectangular porcelain > bowl, in the absence of any visible stressors (like someone striking it with > a sledgehammer)? We hadn't done anything unusual to the toilet in the recent All it takes is an overtorqued nut (e.g. at the water intake entrance) to stress the porcelain, and you've got a time bomb waiting to go off. Of course, if you're lucky, you'll overtorque it enough that the tank will break right away, while you still have the water intake shut off. - Joe",0,20030228_easy_ham.tar.bz2,easy_ham/00334.c7dd12784ce69de4bb49147cce037d76,6fd305ba9070a5899f4683058b2f5e0c3350f9e0b99b8c4790717503a84a65b5
